	affiliations = {GE Grid Solutions, Markham, Canada; Grid Solutions, Stafford, United Kingdom},
	abstract = {Cyber threats to the power grid are on the rise, and attacks are getting sophisticated and more targeted. In 2015, Ukraine attack showed that attacking a distribution system can cause a widespread power outage. From transportation to health services, majority of critical infrastructure is supplied from the distribution networks. In addition, distribution systems have larger attack surface to due geographically dispersed devices. Integration of renewables and Microgrid furthennore introduces more security vulnerabilities to distributed controls over the distribution. This paper first investigates cyber threats and challenges that are specific to Distribution systems. It then discusses cyber security architecture and defence-in-depth security strategies for Distribution system. Then, this paper presents the importance of Intelligent Electronic Device (IED) cyber security features, such as Role Based Access Control (RBAC), Central Authentication and Security event logs, and how to integrate IEDs with third party security systems to provide end-to-end cyber security, such as Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) server, password management systems, firewalls, and Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS). If smart IEDs supports built-in cyber security functions, this will eliminate the need for any external security gateway devices over the geographically distributed controllers in the distribution networks. Lastly, this paper discusses how to bring technology, processes, and people together with security services such as security risk assessment, training, and patch management. © 2020 IET Conference Publications.},
	author_keywords = {Cyber security architecture; Cyber strategy; Cybersecurity; Distribution networks},
	keywords = {Authentication; Computer system firewalls; Distributed parameter control systems; Electric power system protection; Electric power transmission networks; Gateways (computer networks); Information management; Intrusion detection; Microgrids; Mobile telecommunication systems; Outages; Risk assessment; Security systems; Distributed controller; Integration of renewables; Intelligent electronic device; Intrusion Detection Systems; Role-based Access Control; Security information and event management (SIEM); Security risk assessments; Security vulnerabilities; Network security},
