	affiliations = {Pes University, Dept.of Cse, Bangalore, India; Pes University, Dept. of Aiml, Bangalore, India; Pes University, Dept. of Ece, Bangalore, India; Pes University, Dept. of Computer Science, Bangalore, India},
	abstract = {Graylog is an open-source log management platform that enables real-time collection, indexing, and visualization of machine data across distributed systems. A secure, scalable, and containerized logging system is built using Graylog, deployed in a multi-agent architecture. Logs are collected from three Windows host machines using Fluent Bit, NXLog, Winlogbeat, and Metricbeat to ensure comprehensive coverage of system, security, and application-level events. Docker is used to containerize the full stack-including Graylog, Elasticsearch, and MongoDB-for portability and simplified deployment. Transport Layer Security (TLS) is implemented with OpenSSL to establish encrypted communication between log shippers and the Graylog server, ensuring data confidentiality and integrity. Elasticsearch serves as the backend datastore for fast indexing and full-text search, while MongoDB handles configuration and metadata storage. Log collection techniques include event-based forwarding, agent-based harvesting, and stream-based routing of critical Windows event logs. Filtering is applied using Graylog streams and pipeline rules based on event IDs and log types, such as failed logins, system alerts, and user activity logs. Real-time alerting is enabled through Slack integration to notify high-priority events like repeated login failures. Experimental evaluation demonstrates secure and reliable ingestion, parsing, filtering, and visualization of logs from heterogeneous sources with low latency. The final setup offers a robust, production-ready solution for centralized log monitoring, supporting security compliance, operational visibility, and scalable deployment in development and pre-production environments. © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Docker; Graylog; Log Management; Real-time Monitoring; SIEM; TLS Security; Windows Event Logs},
