	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105050606079?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {School of Computing, Telkom University, Bandung, Indonesia},
	abstract = {This study proposes an intrusion detection architecture that integrates a Dionaea honeypot, Stratosphere Linux IPS (SLIPS), and a Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) platform based on Elasticsearch and Kibana in a virtualized environment. The architecture is designed to capture suspicious interactions, analyze attacker behavior, forward structured alert data, and visualize security events through centralized monitoring. The system consists of four virtual machines, namely an attacker node, a honeypot and analysis node, a SIEM node, and a real server node. Controlled attack scenarios were conducted using port scanning, SSH brute-force, and denial-of-service activity. The results show that SLIPS successfully detected all evaluated attack scenarios and generated structured alerts in alerts.json, which were then forwarded by Filebeat to Elasticsearch and visualized in Kibana. The evaluation also examined system performance in terms of CPU usage, RAM usage, and detection latency. The results indicate that Dionaea remained relatively lightweight, SLIPS recorded the highest processing load, and Elasticsearch became the most resource-intensive SIEM component. Filebeat introduced only a small overhead, while Kibana resource usage mainly increased during dashboard rendering. The measured detection delays indicate that the proposed architecture operates in a near real-time manner. These findings show that the integration of honeypot-based interaction capture, SLIPS-based behavioral analysis, and SIEM-based visualization is feasible for building an open-source environment for attacker behavior observation and centralized intrusion monitoring. © 2026 IEEE.},
