	abstract = {Network intrusion detection remains a critical challenge for enterprise security operations, particularly as attack methodologies continue to evolve in sophistication. This paper presents a machine learning-enhanced network intrusion detection system that combines behavioral fingerprinting with multi-component risk scoring to identify SQL injection and brute force attacks in real-time. The proposed architecture employs a three-layer feature engineering approach, extracting behavioral fingerprints for known attack patterns, computing component-specific risk scores and applying gradient-boosted ensemble classification for final risk quantification. Seven classification algorithms were trained on the NSL-KDD benchmark dataset and evaluated on both NSL-KDD test set and a curated enterprise dataset of network requests. The XGBoost model achieved 99.2% accuracy on NSL-KDD and 99.4% on the custom dataset, demonstrating exceptional benchmark performance and real-world transferability. 10-fold cross-validation confirmed model stability with 99.1% (0.6%) mean accuracy. The probability-based risk scores enable configurable alerting thresholds for Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) integration with mean processing latency suitable for real-time deployment scenarios.  © 2026 IEEE.},
