	abstract = {This article describes a predictive intrusion detection system that combines Wazuh SIEM and machine learning technologies to address the growing complexity of modern network threats. The system employs a distributed architecture and Random Forest classification methodology, trained on 1,000,107 network flows across eight distinct attack types, utilizing CICFlowMeter for comprehensive feature extraction from bidirectional network traffic. The framework achieves real-time processing capabilities with an overall accuracy of 92.34%, supported by a feature engineering pipeline that applies logarithmic transformations and correlation-based dimensionality reduction. Integration with Kibana and Elasticsearch enables enterprise-level implementation, offering MITRE ATT&CK framework mappings and advanced visualization capabilities that support security operations teams in rapid threat identification and response. The research demonstrates the viability of hybrid ML-SIEM architectures for contemporary network defense, processing over 1,000 flows per second with minimal latency while maintaining high detection accuracy across diverse attack categories including DDoS, DoS, Botnet, and web-based attacks. System resource utilization remains practical for production deployment, with detection memory below 500 MB and CPU utilization between 45-60% under full operational load. © 2026 IEEE.},
