	abstract = {With the widespread application of big data technology, cybersecurity faces immense challenges, including massive data volumes, alert overload, and the covert nature of threats. Traditional security systems struggle to effectively correlate fragmented information, leading to insufficient awareness of multi-step, persistent attacks. To address this issue, this paper proposes a security graph computational reconstruction model (SGCRM) for big data environments. The model first constructs a baseline security knowledge graph containing multi-dimensional, heterogeneous entities such as assets, vulnerabilities, attackers, and behaviors. Its core innovation lies in a computational reconstruction framework that, rather than simply updating graph data, deeply couples temporal evolution with inference capabilities. Through a graph neural network, it dynamically completes causal chains, infers potential malicious correlations, and reconstructs and quantifies the risk of attack paths. Finally, this paper conducts an empirical analysis and ablation studies based on the public cybersecurity dataset CIC-IDS-2017. The experimental results show that, compared to traditional SIEM systems, static graph analysis, and other dynamic GCN detection models, the proposed SGCRM improves the attack detection rate by up to 12.5% and reduces the mean time to detect by 45.8%, while effectively lowering the false positive rate. The ablation study further confirms the critical contribution of the inference reconstruction module to the model's performance, validating the model's effectiveness in enhancing the proactivity and precision of security defense. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
